  "prompt_text": "The figure schematically summarizes how different excitonic complexes in a semiconductor absorb and emit light, and how their binding energies are defined. Across the panels, electrons are shown in green, holes in white, and an ellipse outlines the bound complex; a vertical arrow indicates optical polarization or dipole direction.\n\nAt the top, a neutral exciton—one electron bound to one hole—recombines and produces photon emission, illustrating the basic radiative transition. The next rows compare this exciton with more complex charged and multicarrier states. A trion appears as an exciton plus an extra electron, and its binding energy is defined by the energy needed to separate it into a neutral exciton and a free electron. A biexciton is shown as two bound electron–hole pairs, with its binding energy referenced to two separate excitons. Another charged multicarrier state combines a trion-like complex with an additional exciton or free carrier, again defining a dissociation energy.\n\nThe bottom panel adds geometry and lattice coupling: the transition dipole is tilted by an angle θ, and a higher-order charged complex can decay into an exciton plus a free electron while coupling to “RO soft phonons.” Overall, the diagram presents a hierarchy of excitonic quasiparticles and the energetic channels connecting them.",
